1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,757
Customers rarely quote a transaction ID.

2
00:00:03,807 --> 00:00:07,285
When a message could match several payments,

3
00:00:07,335 --> 00:00:13,941
an assistant that guesses can give a correct-sounding answer about the wrong money.

4
00:00:13,991 --> 00:00:16,266
Finbot is built not to guess.

5
00:00:17,206 --> 00:00:22,726
A customer of a fictional bank types: "My two-hundred-dollar payment hasn't arrived.

6
00:00:22,776 --> 00:00:26,837
When will it get there?" No date, no payee, no transaction ID.

7
00:00:26,887 --> 00:00:30,219
Their account has four two-hundred-dollar payments,

8
00:00:30,269 --> 00:00:32,739
so the amount alone rules nothing out.

9
00:00:32,789 --> 00:00:37,645
Finbot keeps a probability for each record the customer is allowed to see,

10
00:00:37,695 --> 00:00:42,552
scores each question it could ask by how much it would narrow things down,

11
00:00:42,602 --> 00:00:46,796
and asks the best one: was it a card payment or a bank transfer?

12
00:00:47,756 --> 00:00:50,062
The customer picks bank transfer.

13
00:00:50,112 --> 00:00:55,059
Asked for the date, they name the payee instead, and Finbot uses that.

14
00:00:55,109 --> 00:00:58,129
One record now fits what the customer said,

15
00:00:58,179 --> 00:01:01,841
but a probability never selects a record on its own.

16
00:01:01,891 --> 00:01:04,053
The customer has to confirm it.

17
00:01:05,494 --> 00:01:08,256
Now the confirmed record is fetched again,

18
00:01:08,306 --> 00:01:11,403
and the customer's question goes to the writer.

19
00:01:11,453 --> 00:01:13,947
Here, the draft adds a processing fee.

20
00:01:13,997 --> 00:01:17,562
Plain code, not another model, looks for every amount,

21
00:01:17,612 --> 00:01:20,441
status and ID from the draft in the record.

22
00:01:20,491 --> 00:01:24,993
The fee isn't there, so the draft is rejected, and so is the repair.

23
00:01:25,043 --> 00:01:29,613
The answer is built from the record instead: the transfer is pending,

24
00:01:29,663 --> 00:01:31,554
expected November eighteenth.

25
00:01:31,604 --> 00:01:35,236
An answer that skipped the date would be sent back too.

26
00:01:35,286 --> 00:01:38,048
Each answer is labelled with who wrote it.

27
00:01:39,406 --> 00:01:41,327
Being on the record isn't enough:

28
00:01:41,377 --> 00:01:43,836
each amount must also play the right role.

29
00:01:43,886 --> 00:01:47,539
This draft gives the two-hundred-dollar payment as the refund,

30
00:01:47,589 --> 00:01:50,406
which was fifty, so it is rejected and repaired.

31
00:01:52,114 --> 00:01:57,535
We tested this on 224 real Banking77 questions with the reference removed,

32
00:01:57,585 --> 00:02:01,158
with simulated customers from two model families.

33
00:02:01,208 --> 00:02:06,777
Among eight records, one well-chosen question puts the right record first in

34
00:02:06,827 --> 00:02:12,248
about seven of ten conversations, against two in ten for a fixed question.

35
00:02:12,298 --> 00:02:17,127
Confirmation keeps wrong-record selections at two to four percent,

36
00:02:17,177 --> 00:02:21,119
against over twenty if Finbot acted on its best guess.

37
00:02:22,269 --> 00:02:26,881
Try it in your browser, or run it offline with no API key.
